\begin{abstract}
We synthesize high-resolution spatial and spectral observations of the remnant of Supernova 1987A (\sn) obtained with the \chandra\ between 1999 and 2007 (spanning days $\sim$4500 to $\sim$7500 post-explosion). Over this epoch, the high-velocity supernova blast wave entered a phase of dominant interaction with the dense, clumpy circumstellar equatorial ring (ER) ejected by the progenitor star $\sim 20,000$ years prior to core collapse. The soft X-ray flux ($0.5$--$2.0~\keV$) exhibited a dramatic, non-linear increase—surpassing a factor of five throughout this monitoring period—driven by the progressive impact of the shock wave on dense optical ``hotspots'' within the ER.

Spatially resolved imaging with the Advanced CCD Imaging Spectrometer (ACIS) demonstrates an expanding ring-like morphology whose geometry strongly matches the optical circumstellar ring. Measurement of the X-ray ring's radial expansion yields a shock velocity of $V_{\text{shock}} \approx 3000$--$4000~\kms$ in the ambient inter-knot medium, whereas transmitted shocks entering the high-density ($n_e \gtrsim 10^4~\cm$) optical knots are decelerated to $V_{\text{slow}} \approx 300$--$500~\kms$. High-resolution imaging and grating spectroscopy indicate a multi-temperature thermal plasma best fitted by non-equilibrium ionization (NEI) shock models, comprising a cool shock component ($kT \sim 0.5~\keV$) associated with the slow transmitted shocks and a hot component ($kT \sim 2.0$--$3.0~\keV$) arising from the fast forward shock in lower-density gas. 

Around 2004 (day $\sim 6200$), a structural inflection in the soft X-ray light curve marked the shift from interaction with isolated inward protrusions to full-scale engulfment of the main body of the inner ring. These \chandra\ observations provide critical empirical constraints on the density profile and mass-loss history of the progenitor during its red-to-blue supergiant transition, while establishing a baseline for predicting the ultimate shock-driven destruction of the inner circumstellar ring.
\end{abstract}

