\begin{abstract}
Supernova 1987A (SN 1987A) provides an unprecedented opportunity to observe the early evolution of a young supernova remnant. The \textit{Chandra X-ray Observatory} has continuously monitored SN 1987A since 1999, capturing the dynamic interaction between the supernova ejecta and the circumstellar medium. Our analysis demonstrates a significant X-ray brightening over time, with the 0.5--10 keV luminosity reaching $\sim 2.4 \times 10^{36} \text{ erg s}^{-1}$ by 2007. This dramatic increase in flux is driven by the primary blast wave sweeping into the dense, pre-existing circumstellar equatorial ring. High-resolution X-ray spectral modeling indicates that the emission is dominated by a two-temperature, plane-parallel shock plasma, with characteristic temperatures of $kT \approx 0.3 \text{ keV}$ and $2 \text{ keV}$. Furthermore, radial expansion measurements reveal a sharp deceleration of the X-ray emitting ring; the expansion velocity dropped from an initial blast wave speed of $\sim 6000 \text{ km s}^{-1}$ to $\sim 1400 \text{ km s}^{-1}$ as the shock encountered the high density of the ring material. These results offer key insights into the physics of strong shocks propagating through dense inhomogeneous media and map the mass-loss history of the progenitor star.
\end{abstract}

